<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bay of Fundy</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bay of Fundy is an inlet of the Atlantic between New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, with a small part touching Maine. It has the highest tidal range in the world, reaching about 16 metres. Its shores were the heart of French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acadia">Acadia</a>, where settlers founded Port-Royal in 1605 and drained the tidal marshes with dykes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bay-of-fundy.jpg" alt="Bay of Fundy" width="300" height="230" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Decumanus at English Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wpdms_nasa_topo_bay_of_fundy_-_en.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Bay of Fundy is an arm of the Gulf of Maine and part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean">Atlantic Ocean</a>. Its tidal range is about 16 metres, against an average of about one metre worldwide, because of tidal resonance in its funnel-shaped basin. In one half-day tidal cycle about 100 billion tonnes of water flow in and out, twice the combined flow of all the world's rivers. Most rivers draining into the bay have a tidal bore, among them the Petitcodiac, Maccan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-croix">St. Croix</a> and Kennetcook.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikmaq">Mi'kmaq</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passamaquoddy">Passamaquoddy</a> called the bay Bakudabakek, open way, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliseet">Wolastoqiyik</a> called it Wekwabegituk, waves at the head of the bay. The origin of the name Fundy is debated: suggestions are the French fendu, split, or Fond de la Baie, and, according to William Francis Ganong, a Portuguese source.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikmaq">Mi'kmaq</a> fished in the bay and lived in communities around it for centuries before Europeans arrived. The first European to visit may have been the Portuguese explorer João Álvares Fagundes in about 1520, although the bay does not appear on Portuguese maps until 1558. The first European settlement was French, at Saint Croix Island in Maine and then at Port Royal, founded by Pierre Dugua and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-de-champlain">Samuel de Champlain</a> in 1605. Port Royal was the first permanent European settlement north of St. Augustine in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> and preceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamestown">Jamestown</a> by two years.</p><p>About 75 years later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acadians">Acadians</a> spread along the bay, founding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-pre">Grand-Pré</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beaubassin">Beaubassin</a>, Cobequid and Pisiguit. The settlements saw much military action, first as the French and British fought for control, which led to the expulsion of the Acadians, and later through attacks by Americans in the American Revolution and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-1812">War of 1812</a>.</p><p>In the 19th century the bay was a centre of shipping and shipbuilding. Its ports produced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a>, the fastest ship in the world, and the William D. Lawrence, the largest wooden ship built in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, and Molly Kool, the first female sea captain in the western world, came from there. The highest water level ever recorded, 21.6 metres, occurred in October 1869, when a storm surge from the Saxby Gale coincided with a perigean spring tide, breached dykes in the Minas Basin and the Tantramar Marshes and caused extensive destruction.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The bay lies between the Canadian provinces of New Brunswick and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nova-scotia">Nova Scotia</a>, and a small portion touches Maine. Its largest population centre is Saint John, New Brunswick, a major port and the first incorporated city in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>. Its geology records the break-up of Pangaea in the late Triassic, and its shore has yielded fossils, among them the oldest dinosaur fossil in Canada, found at Burntcoat Head. The bay is a member of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s Global Geoparks Network.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bay of Fundy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1604 Pierre Dugua de Mons and Samuel de Champlain wintered on Saint Croix Island, and in 1605 moved across the <b>Bay of Fundy</b> to found Port-Royal.</blockquote><a href="/state/acadia">Acadia</a></li></ul></section>
